desktop-shell: Windows-style taskbar with a Start menu

Turns the Weston panel into a taskbar, as on Windows:

- Start button (hexaOS logo) opening a Start menu with every [launcher];
  the Super key alone opens it too
- shortcuts on the taskbar for launchers with "pinned=true"
- a button for each open window: click to show it, click again to
  minimize, middle click to close; "app-id=" in a launcher gives its icon
  to matching windows
- right-click menus:
  - window button: open a new window of the app, pin or unpin it,
    Restore, Minimize, Maximize, Close window
  - pinned shortcut: open, Unpin from taskbar
  - Start button: Task Manager, Settings, Terminal, Shut down
  - empty part of the taskbar: Task Manager, Show desktop
  Pins changed this way are saved in ~/.config/hexaos/taskbar-pins
- Ctrl+Shift+Esc opens the Task Manager (/usr/bin/hexa-taskmgr)
- volume (PipeWire, via wpctl): wheel changes it, click mutes
- clock with the date below ("clock-date-format" in [shell])

The shell plugin tells the panel about top-level windows through new
events in weston_desktop_shell version 2 (window, window_removed), and
the panel activates, minimizes, maximizes or closes them with new
requests. The Start menu and the context menus are "popup" surfaces
shown above windows and the panel (one at a time), closed (popup_done)
when the user clicks elsewhere.

Tooltips (toytoolkit) open above the pointer when there is no room
below, so they show on a bottom panel.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [hexaOS specific]

